body:
Closes #110.

GitHub doesn't retry a webhook the service failed to answer, so a push or a thread resolve made while it was down (every deploy, a crash) was never acted on. Now the service asks GitHub to send those again. It does this a minute after startup, once Render routes to the new instance, and every 15 minutes after that.

- It lists the App's deliveries (`GET /app/hook/deliveries`, as the App) and groups the attempts by GUID.
- It redelivers a delivery (`POST …/attempts`), oldest first, when all of these hold:
  - it was first sent in the last 6 hours;
  - no attempt was answered (no response or a 5xx);
  - it was tried fewer than 3 times.
- A 4xx is the service's own rejection (bad signature, bad payload), so it's left alone. It would only fail again.
- Each one logs `redelivered`, or `redelivery failed`, with the GUID, event, original status and time.

The issue's open questions, settled:

- **Duplicates:** the SHA check doesn't cover a label re-review or a re-decision. So `receive` now ignores a delivery whose GUID already started a run (`delivery already received`). That also covers redeliveries made by hand from the App page. `revive()` re-enqueues directly, so it isn't affected.
- **Order:** testing this found a real gap. A `closed` delivery that arrived after a newer `reopened` cancelled the reopened run, and nothing revived it. `recordPrState` now says whether this delivery is the latest word on the PR's state. An older `closed` or `converted_to_draft` cancels nothing. Late pushes were already covered by stale/revive.
- **Lookback:** 6 hours, at most 3 attempts per delivery. The run cap still applies. A longer outage loses its older deliveries, and a busy outage redelivers everything in one pass (marked `ponytail:`).

Tests: `test/redelivery.test.ts` covers:
- which deliveries get redelivered, in what order, and the log lines;
- a delivery older than the window with a recent failed retry;
- a redelivery that fails;
- a repeated GUID on a label event;
- a close and a reopen arriving in reverse order.

The timer wiring in `main.ts` isn't exercised, as with #109.
